1
00:00:01,519 --> 00:00:04,078
Chapter 33.

2
00:00:04,078 --> 00:00:08,277
In her furred travelling-dress, Estella seemed more delicately beautiful than she

3
00:00:08,277 --> 00:00:13,917
had ever seemed yet, even in my eyes. Her manner was more winning than she had cared

4
00:00:13,917 --> 00:00:20,480
to let it be to me before, and I thought I saw Miss Havisham’s influence in the change.

5
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:23,959
We stood in the Inn Yard while she pointed out her luggage to me,

6
00:00:23,961 --> 00:00:27,438
and when it was all collected I remembered—having forgotten everything

7
00:00:27,438 --> 00:00:32,000
but herself in the meanwhile—that I knew nothing of her destination.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,358
“I am going to Richmond,” she told me. “Our lesson is, that there are two Richmonds,

9
00:00:36,358 --> 00:00:42,000
one in Surrey and one in Yorkshire, and that mine is the Surrey Richmond. The distance is

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,758
ten miles. I am to have a carriage, and you are to take me. This is my purse,

11
00:00:47,758 --> 00:00:54,438
and you are to pay my charges out of it. O, you must take the purse! We have no choice,

12
00:00:54,438 --> 00:01:01,277
you and I, but to obey our instructions. We are not free to follow our own devices, you and I.”

13
00:01:01,277 --> 00:01:04,480
As she looked at me in giving me the purse, I hoped there was an

14
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:10,480
inner meaning in her words. She said them slightingly, but not with displeasure.

15
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:15,917
“A carriage will have to be sent for, Estella. Will you rest here a little?”

16
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:17,758
“Yes, I am to rest here a little,

17
00:01:17,758 --> 00:01:22,160
and I am to drink some tea, and you are to take care of me the while.”

18
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:27,199
She drew her arm through mine, as if it must be done, and I requested a waiter

19
00:01:27,199 --> 00:01:31,557
who had been staring at the coach like a man who had never seen such a thing in his life,

20
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:36,839
to show us a private sitting-room. Upon that, he pulled out a napkin,

21
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:41,519
as if it were a magic clue without which he couldn’t find the way upstairs, and led us to the

22
00:01:41,519 --> 00:01:46,680
black hole of the establishment, fitted up with a diminishing mirror (quite a superfluous article,

23
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:53,277
considering the hole’s proportions), an anchovy sauce-cruet, and somebody’s pattens.

24
00:01:53,277 --> 00:01:58,398
On my objecting to this retreat, he took us into another room with a dinner-table for thirty,

25
00:01:58,398 --> 00:02:04,557
and in the grate a scorched leaf of a copy-book under a bushel of coal-dust. Having looked at

26
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:11,160
this extinct conflagration and shaken his head, he took my order; which, proving to be merely,

27
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:16,839
“Some tea for the lady,” sent him out of the room in a very low state of mind.

28
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:23,038
I was, and I am, sensible that the air of this chamber, in its strong combination of stable with

29
00:02:23,038 --> 00:02:29,078
soup-stock, might have led one to infer that the coaching department was not doing well,

30
00:02:29,078 --> 00:02:35,277
and that the enterprising proprietor was boiling down the horses for the refreshment department.

31
00:02:35,277 --> 00:02:42,116
Yet the room was all in all to me, Estella being in it. I thought that with her I could have been

32
00:02:42,116 --> 00:02:50,436
happy there for life. (I was not at all happy there at the time, observe, and I knew it well.)

33
00:02:50,438 --> 00:02:54,480
“Where are you going to, at Richmond?” I asked Estella.

34
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:59,358
“I am going to live,” said she, “at a great expense, with a lady there,

35
00:02:59,358 --> 00:03:04,557
who has the power—or says she has—of taking me about, and introducing me,

36
00:03:04,559 --> 00:03:08,358
and showing people to me and showing me to people.”

37
00:03:08,358 --> 00:03:12,277
“I suppose you will be glad of variety and admiration?”

38
00:03:12,277 --> 00:03:14,800
“Yes, I suppose so.”

39
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:20,519
She answered so carelessly, that I said, “You speak of yourself as if you were some one else.”

40
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:22,917
“Where did you learn how I speak of others?

41
00:03:23,718 --> 00:03:31,319
Come, come,” said Estella, smiling delightfully, “you must not expect me to go to school to you;

42
00:03:31,319 --> 00:03:36,557
I must talk in my own way. How do you thrive with Mr. Pocket?”

43
00:03:36,559 --> 00:03:43,758
“I live quite pleasantly there; at least—” It appeared to me that I was losing a chance.

44
00:03:43,758 --> 00:03:46,559
“At least?” repeated Estella.

45
00:03:46,559 --> 00:03:50,480
“As pleasantly as I could anywhere, away from you.”

46
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:55,639
“You silly boy,” said Estella, quite composedly, “how can you talk such

47
00:03:55,641 --> 00:04:02,038
nonsense? Your friend Mr. Matthew, I believe, is superior to the rest of his family?”

48
00:04:02,038 --> 00:04:05,879
“Very superior indeed. He is nobody’s enemy—”

49
00:04:05,879 --> 00:04:11,917
“Don’t add but his own,” interposed Estella, “for I hate that class of man. But he really

50
00:04:11,917 --> 00:04:17,358
is disinterested, and above small jealousy and spite, I have heard?”

51
00:04:17,358 --> 00:04:20,436
“I am sure I have every reason to say so.”

52
00:04:20,438 --> 00:04:24,398
“You have not every reason to say so of the rest of his people,” said Estella,

53
00:04:24,398 --> 00:04:30,038
nodding at me with an expression of face that was at once grave and rallying, “for they beset Miss

54
00:04:30,038 --> 00:04:36,961
Havisham with reports and insinuations to your disadvantage. They watch you, misrepresent you,

55
00:04:36,961 --> 00:04:42,358
write letters about you (anonymous sometimes), and you are the torment and the occupation

56
00:04:42,358 --> 00:04:48,961
of their lives. You can scarcely realise to yourself the hatred those people feel for you.”

57
00:04:48,961 --> 00:04:52,116
“They do me no harm, I hope?”

58
00:04:52,116 --> 00:04:58,680
Instead of answering, Estella burst out laughing. This was very singular to me,

59
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:04,680
and I looked at her in considerable perplexity. When she left off—and she had not laughed

60
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:10,038
languidly, but with real enjoyment—I said, in my diffident way with her,—

61
00:05:10,038 --> 00:05:15,758
“I hope I may suppose that you would not be amused if they did me any harm.”

62
00:05:15,758 --> 00:05:22,319
“No, no you may be sure of that,” said Estella. “You may be certain that I laugh because they

63
00:05:22,319 --> 00:05:29,718
fail. O, those people with Miss Havisham, and the tortures they undergo!” She laughed again,

64
00:05:29,718 --> 00:05:35,237
and even now when she had told me why, her laughter was very singular to me, for I could

65
00:05:35,237 --> 00:05:41,319
not doubt its being genuine, and yet it seemed too much for the occasion. I thought there must

66
00:05:41,319 --> 00:05:49,597
really be something more here than I knew; she saw the thought in my mind, and answered it.

67
00:05:49,597 --> 00:05:55,680
“It is not easy for even you.” said Estella, “to know what satisfaction it gives me to see those

68
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:02,116
people thwarted, or what an enjoyable sense of the ridiculous I have when they are made ridiculous.

69
00:06:02,116 --> 00:06:08,160
For you were not brought up in that strange house from a mere baby. I was. You had not your

70
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:13,519
little wits sharpened by their intriguing against you, suppressed and defenceless,

71
00:06:13,519 --> 00:06:21,358
under the mask of sympathy and pity and what not that is soft and soothing. I had. You did not

72
00:06:21,358 --> 00:06:26,277
gradually open your round childish eyes wider and wider to the discovery of that impostor

73
00:06:26,277 --> 00:06:33,557
of a woman who calculates her stores of peace of mind for when she wakes up in the night. I did.”

74
00:06:33,559 --> 00:06:36,438
It was no laughing matter with Estella now,

75
00:06:36,438 --> 00:06:41,277
nor was she summoning these remembrances from any shallow place. I would not have

76
00:06:41,277 --> 00:06:46,199
been the cause of that look of hers for all my expectations in a heap.

77
00:06:46,199 --> 00:06:52,961
“Two things I can tell you,” said Estella. “First, notwithstanding the proverb that constant

78
00:06:52,961 --> 00:06:57,597
dropping will wear away a stone, you may set your mind at rest that these

79
00:06:57,597 --> 00:07:03,319
people never will—never would in a hundred years—impair your ground with Miss Havisham,

80
00:07:03,319 --> 00:07:08,678
in any particular, great or small. Second, I am beholden to you as the

81
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:15,038
cause of their being so busy and so mean in vain, and there is my hand upon it.”

82
00:07:15,038 --> 00:07:20,879
As she gave it to me playfully,—for her darker mood had been but momentary—I held it and put

83
00:07:20,879 --> 00:07:28,559
it to my lips. “You ridiculous boy,” said Estella, “will you never take warning? Or

84
00:07:28,559 --> 00:07:33,480
do you kiss my hand in the same spirit in which I once let you kiss my cheek?”

85
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:35,877
“What spirit was that?” said I.

86
00:07:35,879 --> 00:07:41,078
“I must think a moment. A spirit of contempt for the fawners and plotters.”

87
00:07:41,078 --> 00:07:43,961
“If I say yes, may I kiss the cheek again?”

88
00:07:43,961 --> 00:07:49,319
“You should have asked before you touched the hand. But, yes, if you like.”

89
00:07:49,319 --> 00:07:52,639
I leaned down, and her calm face was like a statue’s.

90
00:07:53,758 --> 00:07:58,237
“Now,” said Estella, gliding away the instant I touched her cheek,

91
00:07:58,237 --> 00:08:03,358
“you are to take care that I have some tea, and you are to take me to Richmond.”

92
00:08:03,358 --> 00:08:08,358
Her reverting to this tone as if our association were forced upon us, and we were mere puppets,

93
00:08:08,358 --> 00:08:14,398
gave me pain; but everything in our intercourse did give me pain. Whatever

94
00:08:14,398 --> 00:08:20,436
her tone with me happened to be, I could put no trust in it, and build no hope on it;

95
00:08:20,438 --> 00:08:29,116
and yet I went on against trust and against hope. Why repeat it a thousand times? So it always was.

96
00:08:29,116 --> 00:08:35,160
I rang for the tea, and the waiter, reappearing with his magic clue, brought in by degrees some

97
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:42,319
fifty adjuncts to that refreshment, but of tea not a glimpse. A teaboard, cups and saucers, plates,

98
00:08:42,319 --> 00:08:49,199
knives and forks (including carvers), spoons (various), salt-cellars, a meek little muffin

99
00:08:49,199 --> 00:08:55,000
confined with the utmost precaution under a strong iron cover, Moses in the bulrushes typified by a

100
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,277
soft bit of butter in a quantity of parsley, a pale loaf with a powdered head, two proof

101
00:09:01,277 --> 00:09:07,557
impressions of the bars of the kitchen fireplace on triangular bits of bread, and ultimately a

102
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:14,000
fat family urn; which the waiter staggered in with, expressing in his countenance burden and

103
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:20,116
suffering. After a prolonged absence at this stage of the entertainment, he at length came back with

104
00:09:20,116 --> 00:09:27,078
a casket of precious appearance containing twigs. These I steeped in hot water, and so

105
00:09:27,078 --> 00:09:32,557
from the whole of these appliances extracted one cup of I don’t know what for Estella.

106
00:09:32,559 --> 00:09:38,358
The bill paid, and the waiter remembered, and the ostler not forgotten, and the chambermaid taken

107
00:09:38,358 --> 00:09:46,237
into consideration,—in a word, the whole house bribed into a state of contempt and animosity,

108
00:09:46,237 --> 00:09:53,597
and Estella’s purse much lightened,—we got into our post-coach and drove away. Turning into

109
00:09:53,597 --> 00:10:00,319
Cheapside and rattling up Newgate Street, we were soon under the walls of which I was so ashamed.

110
00:10:00,319 --> 00:10:03,839
“What place is that?” Estella asked me.

111
00:10:03,839 --> 00:10:07,438
I made a foolish pretence of not at first recognising it,

112
00:10:07,438 --> 00:10:13,917
and then told her. As she looked at it, and drew in her head again, murmuring,

113
00:10:13,917 --> 00:10:19,438
“Wretches!” I would not have confessed to my visit for any consideration.

114
00:10:19,438 --> 00:10:24,519
“Mr. Jaggers,” said I, by way of putting it neatly on somebody else,

115
00:10:24,519 --> 00:10:30,199
“has the reputation of being more in the secrets of that dismal place than any man in London.”

116
00:10:30,199 --> 00:10:35,678
“He is more in the secrets of every place, I think,” said Estella, in a low voice.

117
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:39,277
“You have been accustomed to see him often, I suppose?”

118
00:10:39,277 --> 00:10:42,319
“I have been accustomed to see him at uncertain intervals,

119
00:10:42,319 --> 00:10:47,758
ever since I can remember. But I know him no better now, than I did before I could speak

120
00:10:47,758 --> 00:10:54,038
plainly. What is your own experience of him? Do you advance with him?”

121
00:10:54,038 --> 00:10:59,038
“Once habituated to his distrustful manner,” said I, “I have done very well.”

122
00:10:59,038 --> 00:11:05,199
“Are you intimate?” “I have dined with him at his private house.”

123
00:11:05,199 --> 00:11:10,879
“I fancy,” said Estella, shrinking “that must be a curious place.”

124
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:13,277
“It is a curious place.”

125
00:11:13,277 --> 00:11:18,038
I should have been chary of discussing my guardian too freely even with her;

126
00:11:18,038 --> 00:11:22,680
but I should have gone on with the subject so far as to describe the dinner in Gerrard Street,

127
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:28,078
if we had not then come into a sudden glare of gas. It seemed, while it lasted,

128
00:11:28,078 --> 00:11:32,961
to be all alight and alive with that inexplicable feeling I had had before;

129
00:11:32,961 --> 00:11:39,438
and when we were out of it, I was as much dazed for a few moments as if I had been in lightning.

130
00:11:39,438 --> 00:11:43,116
So we fell into other talk, and it was principally about the way by

131
00:11:43,116 --> 00:11:47,680
which we were travelling, and about what parts of London lay on this side of it,

132
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:52,680
and what on that. The great city was almost new to her, she told me,

133
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:57,641
for she had never left Miss Havisham’s neighbourhood until she had gone to France, and

134
00:11:57,641 --> 00:12:04,319
she had merely passed through London then in going and returning. I asked her if my guardian had any

135
00:12:04,319 --> 00:12:11,718
charge of her while she remained here? To that she emphatically said “God forbid!” and no more.

136
00:12:11,718 --> 00:12:15,917
It was impossible for me to avoid seeing that she cared to attract me;

137
00:12:15,917 --> 00:12:22,480
that she made herself winning, and would have won me even if the task had needed pains. Yet

138
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:27,519
this made me none the happier, for even if she had not taken that tone of our being disposed

139
00:12:27,519 --> 00:12:32,641
of by others, I should have felt that she held my heart in her hand because she wilfully chose

140
00:12:32,641 --> 00:12:39,160
to do it, and not because it would have wrung any tenderness in her to crush it and throw it away.

141
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:43,680
When we passed through Hammersmith, I showed her where Mr. Matthew Pocket lived,

142
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:48,961
and said it was no great way from Richmond, and that I hoped I should see her sometimes.

143
00:12:48,961 --> 00:12:52,879
“O yes, you are to see me; you are to come when you think proper;

144
00:12:52,879 --> 00:12:57,839
you are to be mentioned to the family; indeed you are already mentioned.”

145
00:12:57,839 --> 00:13:02,559
I inquired was it a large household she was going to be a member of?

146
00:13:02,559 --> 00:13:08,519
“No; there are only two; mother and daughter. The mother is a lady of some station,

147
00:13:08,519 --> 00:13:12,078
though not averse to increasing her income.”

148
00:13:12,078 --> 00:13:16,319
“I wonder Miss Havisham could part with you again so soon.”

149
00:13:16,319 --> 00:13:20,319
“It is a part of Miss Havisham’s plans for me, Pip,” said Estella,

150
00:13:20,319 --> 00:13:25,237
with a sigh, as if she were tired; “I am to write to her constantly and see her

151
00:13:25,237 --> 00:13:32,078
regularly and report how I go on,—I and the jewels,—for they are nearly all mine now.”

152
00:13:32,078 --> 00:13:37,641
It was the first time she had ever called me by my name. Of course she did so purposely,

153
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:40,597
and knew that I should treasure it up.

154
00:13:40,597 --> 00:13:45,879
We came to Richmond all too soon, and our destination there was a house by the green,—a

155
00:13:45,879 --> 00:13:52,480
staid old house, where hoops and powder and patches, embroidered coats, rolled stockings,

156
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:59,160
ruffles and swords, had had their court days many a time. Some ancient trees before the

157
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:05,680
house were still cut into fashions as formal and unnatural as the hoops and wigs and stiff skirts;

158
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:10,680
but their own allotted places in the great procession of the dead were not far off,

159
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:15,758
and they would soon drop into them and go the silent way of the rest.

160
00:14:15,758 --> 00:14:21,480
A bell with an old voice—which I dare say in its time had often said to the house,

161
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:26,277
Here is the green farthingale, Here is the diamond-hilted sword,

162
00:14:26,277 --> 00:14:32,519
Here are the shoes with red heels and the blue solitaire—sounded gravely in the moonlight,

163
00:14:32,519 --> 00:14:37,237
and two cherry-coloured maids came fluttering out to receive Estella. The

164
00:14:37,237 --> 00:14:44,000
doorway soon absorbed her boxes, and she gave me her hand and a smile, and said good-night,

165
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,199
and was absorbed likewise. And still I stood looking at the house, thinking how happy I should

166
00:14:51,199 --> 00:14:59,277
be if I lived there with her, and knowing that I never was happy with her, but always miserable.

167
00:14:59,277 --> 00:15:02,319
I got into the carriage to be taken back to Hammersmith,

168
00:15:02,319 --> 00:15:08,519
and I got in with a bad heart-ache, and I got out with a worse heart-ache. At our own door,

169
00:15:08,519 --> 00:15:13,839
I found little Jane Pocket coming home from a little party escorted by her little lover;

170
00:15:13,839 --> 00:15:19,398
and I envied her little lover, in spite of his being subject to Flopson.

171
00:15:19,398 --> 00:15:25,078
Mr. Pocket was out lecturing; for, he was a most delightful lecturer on domestic economy,

172
00:15:25,078 --> 00:15:29,199
and his treatises on the management of children and servants were considered the very best

173
00:15:29,199 --> 00:15:35,839
text-books on those themes. But Mrs. Pocket was at home, and was in a little difficulty,

174
00:15:35,839 --> 00:15:39,438
on account of the baby’s having been accommodated with a needle-case to keep

175
00:15:39,438 --> 00:15:44,559
him quiet during the unaccountable absence (with a relative in the Foot Guards) of

176
00:15:44,559 --> 00:15:49,480
Millers. And more needles were missing than it could be regarded as quite wholesome for

177
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:55,800
a patient of such tender years either to apply externally or to take as a tonic.

178
00:15:55,800 --> 00:16:00,718
Mr. Pocket being justly celebrated for giving most excellent practical advice,

179
00:16:00,718 --> 00:16:06,319
and for having a clear and sound perception of things and a highly judicious mind, I had some

180
00:16:06,319 --> 00:16:12,199
notion in my heart-ache of begging him to accept my confidence. But happening to look up at Mrs.

181
00:16:12,199 --> 00:16:17,277
Pocket as she sat reading her book of dignities after prescribing Bed as a sovereign remedy for

182
00:16:17,277 --> 00:16:21,000
baby, I thought—Well—No, I wouldn’t. Chapter 34.
